WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.496
- Nice to see you.
(audience applauds)

00:00:01.496 --> 00:00:02.329
- Nice to see you, too.

00:00:02.329 --> 00:00:05.030
You know, I may not be from
Texas, but my dad actually is.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:05.863
- Oh really?

00:00:05.863 --> 00:00:06.696
Where, do you know?
- So there we go.

00:00:06.696 --> 00:00:09.378
- He's from Johnson County.

00:00:09.378 --> 00:00:10.316
- What?!

00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:11.570
Are you (beep) me right now?

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.403
- [Jackson] No, I'm dead serious.

00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:13.498
- I'm from Johnson and Tarrant.

00:00:13.498 --> 00:00:14.331
- Really?
- Yeah,

00:00:14.331 --> 00:00:15.717
I lived in both growing up, yeah.

00:00:15.717 --> 00:00:17.692
- So, I didn't really
know my father very much

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:18.850
growing up.
- Okay, me neither, ha!

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.076
- Hey, all right.
- High five!

00:00:20.076 --> 00:00:21.120
Hey, therapy!
- But I do know

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:21.953
that's where he's from.

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:23.818
Yeah, exactly. (laughs)
- Yeah, that's awesome.

00:00:23.818 --> 00:00:24.856
- That's where I am right now.

00:00:24.856 --> 00:00:25.738
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- This is therapy, right?

00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:26.571
- This is therapy.

00:00:26.571 --> 00:00:27.603
- Oh, thank god.
- Welcome to my couch, yeah.

00:00:27.603 --> 00:00:28.436
(Jackson laughs)

00:00:28.436 --> 00:00:30.135
I can talk a lot about that issue, but no.

00:00:30.135 --> 00:00:31.482
But you had a mini "Dawson's Creek"

00:00:31.482 --> 00:00:32.864
reunion with Michelle, right,

00:00:32.864 --> 00:00:33.899
at the Oscars?
- I did, yeah.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:34.732
- How is that,
(audience cheers)

00:00:34.732 --> 00:00:36.389
like when you run into each other like-

00:00:36.389 --> 00:00:37.781
- [Audience Member] Pacey!
- [Kelly] Yes, okay.

00:00:37.781 --> 00:00:38.614
- [Jackson] Thank you, darling.

00:00:38.614 --> 00:00:39.447
- Still excited, still very excited.

00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:40.280
It's 20 years, though.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.196
That's a crazy amount of time.

00:00:41.196 --> 00:00:42.029
- 25, I think.

00:00:42.029 --> 00:00:43.063
- Oh, 25?

00:00:43.063 --> 00:00:43.896
- Yeah.
- Wow.

00:00:43.896 --> 00:00:44.729
Over 20 years.

00:00:44.729 --> 00:00:45.562
Look how serious-

00:00:45.562 --> 00:00:46.395
- [Jackson] It was a little bit less

00:00:46.395 --> 00:00:47.360
gray in the beard.
- What is she saying?

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.193
She looks very serious.
- She was saying,

00:00:48.193 --> 00:00:49.783
"I can't believe you're a father!"

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.490
(both laugh)

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:52.544
- No, but that's what's so cool.

00:00:52.544 --> 00:00:54.450
Like, years later, you
knew each other so young.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.283
- [Jackson] Yeah,

00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:56.116
you were the kids.
- And like,

00:00:56.116 --> 00:00:56.949
Yeah!

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:57.782
- Yeah, it's, I mean,

00:00:57.782 --> 00:00:59.425
this, we don't get to see
each other all that often.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:00.258
- Yeah.
- She has her kids.

00:01:00.258 --> 00:01:01.091
She lives in New York.

00:01:01.091 --> 00:01:02.715
I have my kid, we live here.

00:01:02.715 --> 00:01:04.080
But that was a lovely night,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.778
and just having the
opportunity to see her.

00:01:05.778 --> 00:01:07.895
I hadn't met her new husband
since they got together.

00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:08.728
- [Kelly] Oh yeah.

00:01:08.728 --> 00:01:10.215
- [Jackson] Who's the third
person in that photograph.

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:11.048
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:11.048 --> 00:01:12.437
- So, yeah, I mean, it's a
funny thing to get, like,

00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:15.515
the most fancy dress-up party
that you can possibly do

00:01:15.515 --> 00:01:16.704
and then have a high school reunion,

00:01:16.704 --> 00:01:17.537
but that's what we did!

00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:18.370
- Yeah.
(Jackson laughs)

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.203
I love that, though.

00:01:19.203 --> 00:01:20.036
It's fun.
- Yeah.

00:01:20.036 --> 00:01:20.981
- You actually did "Mighty Ducks"

00:01:20.981 --> 00:01:22.433
years before "Dawson's Creek" and that,

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:23.458
is that hard?

00:01:23.458 --> 00:01:25.810
Like, I just think, whenever I was a kid

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.388
I did not want to stand out.
(Jackson laughs)

00:01:27.388 --> 00:01:28.471
And like, when you're an actor

00:01:28.471 --> 00:01:30.162
in such a huge movie like that

00:01:30.162 --> 00:01:30.999
and then you go back to school,

00:01:30.999 --> 00:01:33.366
was it cool, or did people pick on you?

00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:34.578
- It was a little bit of both.

00:01:34.578 --> 00:01:35.411
- Okay.

00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:36.514
- So one, I'm Canadian,
(audience cheers)

00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:37.849
so hockey, obviously.
- [Kelly] Oh, people are nice.

00:01:37.849 --> 00:01:38.682
- Oh no,

00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:40.558
we take hockey really, really seriously

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.217
- No, I know, but people are nice there.

00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:43.779
There's no mean kids, right?

00:01:43.779 --> 00:01:44.612
I feel like you're all nice.

00:01:44.612 --> 00:01:45.463
- I hate to break it to you, but-

00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:46.969
- Don't burst that bubble for us.

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:48.739
- Yeah, we are all nice, all the time.

00:01:48.739 --> 00:01:49.721
- Okay, thank you.
- Yeah.

00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:50.554
- Thank you.

00:01:50.554 --> 00:01:51.387
- Yeah, high school, you know,

00:01:51.387 --> 00:01:52.381
it had its challenges, definitely.

00:01:52.381 --> 00:01:53.465
- Yeah.
- And going away,

00:01:53.465 --> 00:01:55.374
you know, first off you
disappear for a couple months

00:01:55.374 --> 00:01:56.503
and nobody knows why you're gone.

00:01:56.503 --> 00:01:57.336
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And then you come back

00:01:57.336 --> 00:01:58.391
and nobody cares why you were gone.

00:01:58.391 --> 00:01:59.867
And then the movie comes
out and they're like,

00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:01.143
"So you think you're special now?"

00:02:01.143 --> 00:02:02.066
- That's what I mean.
(Jackson laughs)

00:02:02.066 --> 00:02:03.191
Like, kids can be so hard.

00:02:03.191 --> 00:02:04.024
- [Jackson] Yeah, kids are tough.

00:02:04.024 --> 00:02:05.510
- Yeah.
- But on the flip side of it,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.317
you know, for me, I think acting
can be a difficult journey

00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:09.559
for a lot of young people.

00:02:09.559 --> 00:02:10.392
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But for me,

00:02:10.392 --> 00:02:12.720
it gave me a place to focus

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.744
and to have something that
grown-ups would respect me for.

00:02:15.744 --> 00:02:16.577
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- You know, if I went

00:02:16.577 --> 00:02:18.416
and I worked hard and I
showed up and knew my stuff-

00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:19.287
- Responsible.
- I could actually

00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:20.715
get respect from grownups,

00:02:20.715 --> 00:02:22.658
which you definitely don't in high school.

00:02:22.658 --> 00:02:23.491
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- You know,

00:02:23.491 --> 00:02:24.324
you're the problem.

00:02:24.324 --> 00:02:26.023
So, it actually, for me, was a really

00:02:26.023 --> 00:02:27.970
healthy release and place to focus.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.707
Even if not all of my fellow
students enjoyed my success.

00:02:31.707 --> 00:02:32.828
- Yeah, who cares?
(Jackson laughs)

00:02:32.828 --> 00:02:34.036
They're jealous.

00:02:34.036 --> 00:02:35.940
So wait, do you remember
how you found out,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:36.807
like when you got the role?

00:02:36.807 --> 00:02:38.638
That's a huge role for a kid.
- Yeah, so-

00:02:38.638 --> 00:02:39.471
- Or anybody.

00:02:39.471 --> 00:02:43.134
- The, in Vancouver
public elementary school,

00:02:43.134 --> 00:02:44.958
there's these things
called "mini schools."

00:02:44.958 --> 00:02:47.956
So the mini schools, like, one
is called (indistinct) TREK,

00:02:47.956 --> 00:02:49.587
that takes, you get to be outside.

00:02:49.587 --> 00:02:50.932
Anyway, I went to the art school

00:02:50.932 --> 00:02:52.265
'cause I wanted to be an actor.

00:02:52.265 --> 00:02:53.870
- Yeah.
- So, one of the things

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.214
that you have to do at the mini schools,

00:02:55.214 --> 00:02:56.047
'cause you're in public school,

00:02:56.047 --> 00:02:58.072
is raise money to pay for
anything that you want.

00:02:58.072 --> 00:02:59.895
So every year you do these phone drives.

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:02.990
So I was, like, in the
utility closet at the school,

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.076
and my mom called through
the central switchboard

00:03:05.076 --> 00:03:06.964
to be like, "Hey, guess what?
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:06.964 --> 00:03:08.215
You got the movie!"

00:03:08.215 --> 00:03:09.406
as I'm in the middle of being like,

00:03:09.406 --> 00:03:11.033
"Hi, would you like to donate $5 to,

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.649
to Ideal Mini?"
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:14.926
- From your janitor closet?
- Yeah, exactly.

00:03:14.926 --> 00:03:17.624
So that's the glamorous
space that my career started.

00:03:17.624 --> 00:03:18.878
- I love that.
(Jackson laughs)

00:03:18.878 --> 00:03:19.758
That's so cool.

00:03:19.758 --> 00:03:21.198
So, I just need to verify something-

00:03:21.198 --> 00:03:22.265
- [Jackson] Mhm.
- From Wikipedia

00:03:22.265 --> 00:03:23.473
that we found out.

00:03:23.473 --> 00:03:25.229
Is it true that you were
kicked out of school

00:03:25.229 --> 00:03:27.240
because of the Jon Stewart show?

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.073
Is this-

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:29.113
- Yes.
- He's very powerful.

00:03:29.113 --> 00:03:30.196
- So, he is.
- Yeah.

00:03:30.196 --> 00:03:31.069
- Wickedly powerful.
- Yeah.

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:32.100
I like him a lot.
- You have no idea.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:32.933
Me too!
- Yeah.

00:03:32.933 --> 00:03:33.766
- I like him too much.

00:03:33.766 --> 00:03:34.959
That's why I got kicked
out of high school.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:35.792
- Oh!

00:03:35.792 --> 00:03:37.156
- So, it's actually the second time

00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:38.724
I got kicked out of high school.

00:03:38.724 --> 00:03:39.930
That's a different story.
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:40.763
- [Kelly] Oh, okay.

00:03:40.763 --> 00:03:41.596
- My mother's gonna be so proud

00:03:41.596 --> 00:03:43.705
that I'm telling this story.
- So proud.

00:03:43.705 --> 00:03:46.095
- So after I left Ideal Mini,

00:03:46.095 --> 00:03:48.170
after they politely
requested that I leave,

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.991
I went to my local high school
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:49.991 --> 00:03:51.448
which was Kits High School,

00:03:51.448 --> 00:03:53.812
and Jon Stewart's show was
on at two in the morning,

00:03:53.812 --> 00:03:55.495
local time, Vancouver.
- Yeah.

00:03:55.495 --> 00:03:57.471
- So I would stay up to watch Jon Stewart,

00:03:57.471 --> 00:03:59.117
and it was also during the OJ trial.

00:03:59.117 --> 00:04:00.825
So then in the morning, I'd be tired,

00:04:00.825 --> 00:04:02.269
I'd flip on the TV, I'd be like,

00:04:02.269 --> 00:04:03.791
"Maybe I'll just watch
a couple hours of OJ

00:04:03.791 --> 00:04:06.116
instead of going to the
first three classes."

00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:06.968
And eventually, they thought
(audience laughs)

00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:08.717
that was not a good
educational program for me,

00:04:08.717 --> 00:04:10.319
and they asked me to
leave that school, too.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:11.693
- Oh my gosh!
- Yeah.

00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:13.374
Jon Stewart and OJ got me
kicked outta high school.

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:14.207
- But look at you now.

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:15.631
Your mom has to be proud of you.

00:04:15.631 --> 00:04:17.069
- (laughs) Yeah.
- Look at you now.

00:04:17.069 --> 00:04:18.078
So, is it true
(audience applauds)

00:04:18.078 --> 00:04:19.642
that you live in your childhood home?

00:04:19.642 --> 00:04:21.247
- [Jackson] Yeah.
- That is crazy cool.

00:04:21.247 --> 00:04:22.080
- Yeah.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:22.913
- How did that come about?

00:04:22.913 --> 00:04:24.314
It hasn't been, has it been
in your family the whole time?

00:04:24.314 --> 00:04:25.279
Or did you have to, like, buy it back?

00:04:25.279 --> 00:04:27.204
- No, so that father that
I didn't know very well.

00:04:27.204 --> 00:04:28.526
- Yeah.
- When my parents got divorced

00:04:28.526 --> 00:04:30.010
he sold the house.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.800
Flash forward many, many, many years.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.639
I was in the neighborhood
that that house was in,

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:36.068
and a friend of mine was
getting married there.

00:04:36.068 --> 00:04:38.570
And the day after, I went
to go see the old house,

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:40.931
and the guy who had
bought it from my father

00:04:40.931 --> 00:04:42.589
was standing outside on his porch.

00:04:42.589 --> 00:04:43.431
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And thank god,

00:04:43.431 --> 00:04:44.442
I was with my friend Daniel.

00:04:44.442 --> 00:04:45.871
And he called up and was like,

00:04:45.871 --> 00:04:47.294
"Hey, you know, this is
my friend's old house."

00:04:47.294 --> 00:04:48.127
And I looked at it, I was like,

00:04:48.127 --> 00:04:51.066
"Man, I put that sticker in
the window when I was a kid."

00:04:51.066 --> 00:04:53.293
And he'd left this sticker
in my childhood bedroom.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:55.965
And he goes, "Oh, you mean
up in the dragon room?"

00:04:55.965 --> 00:04:57.972
And the dragon room is
my childhood bedroom

00:04:57.972 --> 00:05:00.047
that had this big mural
of a dragon painted on it

00:05:00.047 --> 00:05:01.722
by my mother's friend.
- Yeah.

00:05:01.722 --> 00:05:03.298
- Because when I was a kid,
I was obsessed with dragons.

00:05:03.298 --> 00:05:05.364
So long story short, at the end of, like,

00:05:05.364 --> 00:05:07.027
a seven-hour conversation, I said,

00:05:07.027 --> 00:05:09.127
"Hey man, if you ever
consider selling this house,

00:05:09.127 --> 00:05:10.650
please let me try buying it."

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.116
And I, we're talking 20 years ago now.

00:05:12.116 --> 00:05:13.602
- Yeah.
- And

00:05:13.602 --> 00:05:15.815
he called me up the next morning
at 9 A.M. and he was like,

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.539
"Man, it just feels right.

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:18.372
Were you serious?"

00:05:18.372 --> 00:05:19.631
I was like, "Absolutely!"

00:05:19.631 --> 00:05:20.890
So I bought that house.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.938
And I thought that I was buying it for me,

00:05:22.938 --> 00:05:25.156
to heal that wound of my family rupture.

00:05:25.156 --> 00:05:26.639
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But it turns out,

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:28.239
I was just holding onto it for 20 years,

00:05:28.239 --> 00:05:30.050
because let me tell
you, taking my baby girl

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.940
and putting her into
the crib in my bedroom

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.362
for the first time-
- Aw, the same-

00:05:33.362 --> 00:05:35.002
- Under the dragon?
(audience awws)

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:36.922
(Jackson exhales)
- That's really powerful.

00:05:36.922 --> 00:05:38.158
- Yeah.
(audience applauds)

00:05:38.158 --> 00:05:39.023
- Yeah.

00:05:39.023 --> 00:05:40.020
I didn't have that either.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.073
A friend of mine has this song

00:05:42.073 --> 00:05:43.258
called "The House That Built Me",

00:05:43.258 --> 00:05:44.930
and I was listening to
it, and I cried so hard.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.264
I was like, "Not everybody gets this."

00:05:46.264 --> 00:05:47.097
- [Jackson] Yeah, no, it's-

00:05:47.097 --> 00:05:47.997
- Like, not everybody knows that,

00:05:47.997 --> 00:05:49.749
that feeling, or you've moved a lot or-

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:50.637
- And for us, yeah.

00:05:50.637 --> 00:05:52.468
It was just, you know,

00:05:52.468 --> 00:05:54.639
all divorces are troublesome,

00:05:54.639 --> 00:05:55.472
but, the-
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:55.472 --> 00:05:56.676
They're, you know, from experience,

00:05:56.676 --> 00:05:57.509
yeah, they're not fun.

00:05:57.509 --> 00:05:58.342
- Yeah, not great.

00:05:58.342 --> 00:05:59.175
Right.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:59.175 --> 00:06:00.008
- Right, right.

00:06:00.008 --> 00:06:02.329
So, when my parents split,
my dad, unfortunately,

00:06:02.329 --> 00:06:04.564
wasn't a very good man, and
he just sort of took off.

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:05.850
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And so,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.219
we had this major rupture.

00:06:07.219 --> 00:06:08.434
And to be able to come full circle

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:09.865
for myself, into this house, but now

00:06:09.865 --> 00:06:11.866
to be a father in this place?

00:06:11.866 --> 00:06:12.856
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Like, it is

00:06:12.856 --> 00:06:17.224
just magical to witness
her experience all the,

00:06:17.224 --> 00:06:19.342
the joys that I remember from that place.

00:06:19.342 --> 00:06:20.855
- Yeah.
- And be able to

00:06:20.855 --> 00:06:22.340
actually be in the other seat now,

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.576
and know that I'm gonna provide her

00:06:23.576 --> 00:06:25.421
with a real man and a
real father in her life.

00:06:25.421 --> 00:06:26.553
- And that's the coolest part, right?

00:06:26.553 --> 00:06:27.508
Is, I feel that too.

00:06:27.508 --> 00:06:30.163
I never really realized
until I had my own kids that,

00:06:30.163 --> 00:06:31.847
what a loss I really-
- Yeah.

00:06:31.847 --> 00:06:33.711
- Like what a loss it was.
- A hundred percent.

00:06:33.711 --> 00:06:35.140
- And then you have kids, and I'm like,

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:36.266
and it's almost like, you know what,

00:06:36.266 --> 00:06:38.660
thank you for showing
me, like, what not to do.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:39.493
- [Jackson] Yeah.
- Like, you know,

